        "title": "Laser Experiment Prioritization",
        "difficulty": "medium",
        "description": "<p>A space agency's laser lab conducts experiments for satellite missions. Recent data corruption in experiment codes requires cleaning while prioritizing urgent missions. The operations team needs a concise report of critical experiments with sanitized codes. &nbsp;</p>\n<p>You are given two tables: 'missions', 'experiments': &nbsp;</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The 'missions' table includes columns 'mission_id', 'mission_name', and 'priority', representing the mission's unique ID, its designation, and urgency level ('Critical', 'High', 'Medium', 'Low'). &nbsp;</li>\n<li>The 'experiments' table includes columns 'exp_id', 'mission_id', 'exp_code', and 'run_date', representing the experiment's unique ID, associated mission ID, corrupted experiment code (containing 'ERR_' prefix), and execution date. &nbsp;</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Your task is to write a SQL query that: &nbsp;</p>\n<ul>\n<li data-start=\"390\" data-end=\"455\">Focus on missions whose priority is <strong data-start=\"428\" data-end=\"440\">Critical</strong> or <strong data-start=\"444\" data-end=\"452\">High</strong>.</li>\n<li data-start=\"458\" data-end=\"540\">For each such mission, identify its three most recent experiments by run_date.</li>\n<li data-start=\"543\" data-end=\"606\">Clean each experiment&rsquo;s code by removing the prefix <code data-start=\"595\" data-end=\"603\">'ERR_'</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The final output should include the columns 'mission_name', 'clean_exp_code', and 'run_date', ordered by run_date descending and mission_name ascending.</p>",
