        "description": "<p>The skydiving school analyzes jump records to identify instructors conducting jumps from uncommon heights for specialized training recognition. &nbsp;</p>\n<p>You are given two tables: 'instructors' and 'jumps': &nbsp;</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The 'instructors' table includes the columns 'instructor_id', 'instructor_name', and 'specialization', representing unique instructor identifiers, instructor names, and their specialized skydiving disciplines. &nbsp;</li>\n<li>The 'jumps' table includes the columns 'jump_id', 'instructor_id', 'student_id', 'jump_date', and 'jump_height_ft', representing unique jump identifiers, instructor identifiers, student identifiers, jump dates, and jump heights in feet. &nbsp;</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Your task is to write a SQL query that:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Determines the jump height (in feet) that ranks third from the bottom when all jump heights are ordered by ascending frequency (and by height ascending to break ties).</li>\n<li>Finds all instructors who have conducted at least one jump at that identified height.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The final output should include the columns 'instructor_name' and 'specialization', sorted by instructor_name in ascending order.</p>",
